00:02:55.510 --> 00:02:57.020
♪ Come on, Jeff, get 'em ♪

00:02:57.020 --> 00:03:01.210
But "The Inside Outtakes" is
actually a bit more inside

00:03:01.210 --> 00:03:04.000
than "Inside" was, and
what I mean by that is,

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:06.360
the outtakes and the alts
and the weird fragments

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:08.850
and the grainy videos
of spiders are giving us

00:03:08.850 --> 00:03:11.510
a lot more of an isolated
and personal take

00:03:11.510 --> 00:03:13.360
on what Burnham was
seemingly going through

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:14.750
during the creation of "Inside,"

00:03:14.750 --> 00:03:16.780
and I'm using the word
seemingly intentionally

00:03:16.780 --> 00:03:18.980
because it's always hard
to tell where the boundary

00:03:18.980 --> 00:03:22.080
between existence and
performance is for this guy.

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:24.900
Whereas "Inside's"
songs focused a lot more

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:27.820
on the digital trends and cultural forces

00:03:27.820 --> 00:03:30.070
making things feel
really bad in the world,

00:03:30.070 --> 00:03:31.520
on "The Inside Outtakes,"

00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:33.490
a lot of these new songs focus more

00:03:33.490 --> 00:03:36.190
on feeling bad and sad and depressed

00:03:36.190 --> 00:03:37.900
at the individual level.

00:03:37.900 --> 00:03:40.240
And if I can make a
really absurd comparison

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:44.360
between Burnham's guesthouse
and Plato's famous Cave,

00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:46.200
it seems like in the outtakes,

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:47.970
Burnham is highlighting the fact

00:03:47.970 --> 00:03:50.640
that understanding how the world works

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:52.640
doesn't necessarily make you any happier,

00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:56.550
and in fact, really understanding
what's going on around you

00:03:56.550 --> 00:03:59.170
might do the opposite and
make you pretty bummed.

00:03:59.170 --> 00:04:01.490
Now, I got the chance to watch
it a couple of times now,

00:04:01.490 --> 00:04:04.860
and I think if there is a kind
of implicit thesis statement

00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:07.160
or thematic to the outtakes, we see it

00:04:07.160 --> 00:04:10.432
in one of the first new songs,
which is called "The Future."

00:04:10.432 --> 00:04:13.064
♪ Wake up every morning
at a quarter to 10 ♪

00:04:13.064 --> 00:04:14.922
♪ Like, am I dying, is this real ♪

00:04:14.922 --> 00:04:17.010
♪ All right, it's this again ♪
He's not feeling very good.

00:04:17.010 --> 00:04:19.925
♪ I'm awoken by my daughter
as she jumps in my bed ♪

00:04:19.925 --> 00:04:23.520
♪ I'm only kidding, I don't
have a kid, I crochet instead ♪

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:26.800
He's lying about a kid that he
doesn't have, which is good.

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:27.633
I mean, he's depressed.

00:04:27.633 --> 00:04:30.510
During the song, Bo is
singing about the future,

00:04:30.510 --> 00:04:32.380
and he seems depressed,
and maybe he's depressed

00:04:32.380 --> 00:04:35.749
because of the future or
more darkly the lack thereof.

00:04:35.749 --> 00:04:37.939
♪ Am I depressed, stressed ♪
♪ Yeah, yeah ♪

00:04:37.939 --> 00:04:39.156
♪ It's anyone's guess ♪

00:04:39.156 --> 00:04:42.360
♪ I'll bother getting better
when I bother getting dressed ♪

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:44.760
Philosophers have long
talked about the experience

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:48.500
of being, to put it in highbrow
terms, super bummed out,

00:04:48.500 --> 00:04:51.460
and whether it's because
they're being executed

00:04:51.460 --> 00:04:53.950
by the state, like Socrates, or struggling

00:04:53.950 --> 00:04:55.950
to find a rational basis
for their own existence,

00:04:55.950 --> 00:04:58.560
like Descartes, or barely coping

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:00.600
with the cultural collapse
of meaning and truth,

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:02.810
like our old pals
Kierkegaard and Nietzsche,

00:05:02.810 --> 00:05:04.117
philosophers have historically

00:05:04.117 --> 00:05:05.900
had lots of reasons to be bummed.

00:05:05.900 --> 00:05:08.160
And there's a contemporary
Italian philosopher

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:10.760
and theorist named Franco "Bifo" Berardi

00:05:10.760 --> 00:05:13.400
who's written about
this and how in our era

00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:15.980
the seeming lack of, like, a good future

00:05:15.980 --> 00:05:17.320
that we're heading towards has led

00:05:17.320 --> 00:05:20.110
to a pretty big wave of
depression and exhaustion.

00:05:20.110 --> 00:05:24.490
For Berardi, depression can be
defined as "a lack of sense,

00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:27.490
as an inability to find
sense through action,

00:05:27.490 --> 00:05:29.200
through communication, through life.

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:32.130
The inability to find
sense is, first of all,

00:05:32.130 --> 00:05:33.650
the inability to create it."

00:05:33.650 --> 00:05:35.950
And finding sense in this context

00:05:35.950 --> 00:05:38.150
is what philosophy aims to do, right?

00:05:38.150 --> 00:05:40.690
Philosophy wants to
make sense of situations

00:05:40.690 --> 00:05:43.690
that are complicated and
confusing and contradictory.

00:05:43.690 --> 00:05:46.920
But what Berardi's saying is
that when we can't make sense

00:05:46.920 --> 00:05:48.990
or find sense in a particular situation,

00:05:48.990 --> 00:05:52.420
like maybe the world of
digital media and capitalism,

00:05:52.420 --> 00:05:54.570
we struggle to create
and communicate sense.

00:05:54.570 --> 00:05:56.610
And here, and throughout these outtakes,

00:05:56.610 --> 00:05:57.770
Burnham seems to be saying

00:05:57.770 --> 00:06:00.610
that contemporary life
doesn't make a lot of sense

00:06:00.610 --> 00:06:02.450
and it's hard to make art and comedy

00:06:02.450 --> 00:06:04.550
about a situation that doesn't make sense

00:06:04.550 --> 00:06:06.310
and that this is all depressing.

00:06:06.310 --> 00:06:09.530
Now, Berardi goes on to argue
that "depression allows us

00:06:09.530 --> 00:06:12.320
to see what we normally
hide from ourselves

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:13.980
through the continuous circulation

00:06:13.980 --> 00:06:15.820
of a reassuring collective narrative.

00:06:15.820 --> 00:06:17.790
Depression sees what
public discourse hides.

00:06:17.790 --> 00:06:20.600
Depression is the best
condition to access the void.

00:06:20.600 --> 00:06:21.980
That is the ultimate truth.

00:06:21.980 --> 00:06:24.780
At the same time, though,
depression paralyzes any ability

00:06:24.780 --> 00:06:26.770
to act, to communicate, to share."

00:06:26.770 --> 00:06:29.160
This also feels like a good way to explore

00:06:29.160 --> 00:06:30.790
and explain what Burnham's doing here

00:06:30.790 --> 00:06:35.060
because a type of depression
and grimness gets one closer

00:06:35.060 --> 00:06:37.170
to the truth of how a situation really is,

00:06:37.170 --> 00:06:39.920
but then you can't communicate
the truth of that thing,

00:06:39.920 --> 00:06:41.730
which is kind of a pickle to be in

00:06:41.730 --> 00:06:44.340
and a vibe I definitely got in "Inside"

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:47.440
and I think we're seeing
throughout this special as well.

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:50.320
Now, Bo is depressed, right?

00:06:50.320 --> 00:06:52.850
And presumably, if you're
depressed, you feel bad,

00:06:52.850 --> 00:06:55.460
and then you would wanna feel good,

00:06:55.460 --> 00:06:57.620
and right here on this
social media platform,

00:06:57.620 --> 00:07:00.830
for every one video of
charming cultural analysis,

00:07:00.830 --> 00:07:03.320
there's, like, 100 videos
that will tell you how

00:07:03.320 --> 00:07:06.290
to feel better or be more productive, um,

00:07:06.290 --> 00:07:08.670
cure your anxiety, your ADHD or whatever,

00:07:08.670 --> 00:07:10.970
and it seems like that's the sort of stuff

00:07:10.970 --> 00:07:14.140
that Bo is looking for in this next song,

00:07:14.140 --> 00:07:17.222
which is about how, damn it,
he just wants to feel good.

00:07:17.222 --> 00:07:19.702
♪ Can you teach how to feel good ♪

00:07:19.702 --> 00:07:20.557
The music's pretty,

00:07:20.557 --> 00:07:22.007
but what he's saying,
♪ I just wanna feel good ♪

00:07:22.007 --> 00:07:24.019
what he's saying is sad.

00:07:24.019 --> 00:07:24.852
♪ I just wanna ♪
♪ I just wanna feel good ♪

00:07:24.852 --> 00:07:26.600
You know, kinda desperate to feel good.

00:07:26.600 --> 00:07:29.390
Now, building off the depressive
vibes we've already seen,

00:07:29.390 --> 00:07:31.750
he's begging for some help to feel good,

00:07:31.750 --> 00:07:34.000
which gets us to a bit of
a contradiction, right?

00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:37.040
Does Burnham want to
have truth or happiness?

00:07:37.040 --> 00:07:39.330
Because as we saw in those Berardi quotes,

00:07:39.330 --> 00:07:41.410
truth can sometimes accompany feelings

00:07:41.410 --> 00:07:42.930
of depression and despair.

00:07:42.930 --> 00:07:45.600
Like, seeing how things
really are isn't always good

00:07:45.600 --> 00:07:48.690
'cause sometimes how
things really are is bad.

00:07:48.690 --> 00:07:50.450
And while there's been
philosophers, like Aristotle,

00:07:50.450 --> 00:07:53.050
who associate truth and
purpose with happiness,

00:07:53.050 --> 00:07:54.630
he doesn't mean, like, feeling good.

00:07:54.630 --> 00:07:57.080
It means more acting
and living in accordance

00:07:57.080 --> 00:07:58.440
with one's nature or purpose.

00:07:58.440 --> 00:08:00.730
And I think this tension
is all over these outtakes

00:08:00.730 --> 00:08:01.650
and the original special.

00:08:01.650 --> 00:08:05.310
Does Bo want to find meaning
in everything that's happening

00:08:05.310 --> 00:08:07.070
at the risk of that
meaning being depressing

00:08:07.070 --> 00:08:08.650
or does he wanna feel good,

00:08:08.650 --> 00:08:11.197
and by extension, do we want him

00:08:11.197 --> 00:08:14.350
to potentially make us feel
bad on the way to meaning

00:08:14.350 --> 00:08:17.700
or do we want the funny
song man to make us laugh?

00:08:17.700 --> 00:08:19.480
Now, of course, over the past year,

00:08:19.480 --> 00:08:21.670
we've seen more and more
answers to his question,

00:08:21.670 --> 00:08:23.450
how do I feel good, all
over the Internet, right?

00:08:23.450 --> 00:08:26.360
Whether it's online therapy,
online psychiatric pharmacies,

00:08:26.360 --> 00:08:28.870
self-help podcasts, meme
accounts, online courses,

00:08:28.870 --> 00:08:30.520
happiness is an industry, and of course,

00:08:30.520 --> 00:08:31.890
and I think Burnham gets this,

00:08:31.890 --> 00:08:34.550
there's an industry for
happiness on the Internet,

00:08:34.550 --> 00:08:36.660
the Internet is the thing
that makes us unhappy...

00:08:36.660 --> 00:08:37.493
Oh, boy.

00:08:37.493 --> 00:08:39.340
Understanding why things are so bad

00:08:39.340 --> 00:08:40.950
doesn't make you feel better

00:08:40.950 --> 00:08:43.740
any more than understanding
why you're sick, right?

00:08:43.740 --> 00:08:45.380
It doesn't magically make you feel better.

00:08:45.380 --> 00:08:49.610
So maybe the best medicine
is laughter. Probably not.

00:08:49.610 --> 00:08:53.050
- I used to perform comedy on a stage,

00:08:53.050 --> 00:08:57.213
um, in front of, uh,
people, living humans,

00:08:58.520 --> 00:09:03.440
and I would try to be funny.

00:09:03.440 --> 00:09:04.762
- Trying to be funny is rough, you know?

00:09:04.762 --> 00:09:06.510
You wanna be, like, naturally
funny. Trying's hard.

00:09:06.510 --> 00:09:10.010
- And then the audience
would sometimes laugh,

00:09:10.010 --> 00:09:11.370
and that was really helpful,

00:09:11.370 --> 00:09:16.080
because I would hear their laughter,

00:09:16.080 --> 00:09:18.210
and that would let me know

00:09:18.210 --> 00:09:21.830
that what I was doing was funny to them.

00:09:21.830 --> 00:09:23.020
- All right, he's doing
something interesting here.

00:09:23.020 --> 00:09:25.810
He's basically getting at
another aspect of loneliness

00:09:25.810 --> 00:09:28.330
and isolation, which is that in his case,

00:09:28.330 --> 00:09:30.400
it's hard to make comedy alone

00:09:30.400 --> 00:09:32.600
'cause he's dependent on laughter.

00:09:32.600 --> 00:09:34.770
This is important because in this sense,

00:09:34.770 --> 00:09:37.480
comedy reflects the intersubjective nature

00:09:37.480 --> 00:09:38.580
of human existence, right?

00:09:38.580 --> 00:09:41.160
We're dependent on other people

00:09:41.160 --> 00:09:44.000
to arrive at ideas, to create meaning,

00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:46.500
even to have, like, a full
conception of ourselves,

00:09:46.500 --> 00:09:48.250
and it seems like what
he's getting at as well

00:09:48.250 --> 00:09:50.290
is that in a state of quarantine

00:09:50.290 --> 00:09:52.510
or just state of our modern digital life,

00:09:52.510 --> 00:09:55.510
there's a lot of
atomization and alienation

00:09:55.510 --> 00:09:57.440
in all these digital spaces, right?

00:09:57.440 --> 00:10:00.160
And the Internet has
this effect of making us

00:10:00.160 --> 00:10:02.410
both atomized and isolated individuals

00:10:02.410 --> 00:10:05.470
and part of one huge, homogenous dataset

00:10:05.470 --> 00:10:06.930
all at the same time

00:10:06.930 --> 00:10:09.380
so that rather than being
fully formed individuals

00:10:09.380 --> 00:10:12.160
existing in some dialectical
relationship with each other,

00:10:12.160 --> 00:10:14.080
we're just isolated units screaming

00:10:14.080 --> 00:10:16.310
into a digital void all at the same time.

00:10:16.310 --> 00:10:17.143
Sounds fun.

00:10:17.143 --> 00:10:19.280
So what do we do if
we're lonely, isolated,

00:10:19.280 --> 00:10:20.800
looking for friends that understand us?

00:10:20.800 --> 00:10:22.650
We listen to podcasts, of course.

00:10:22.650 --> 00:10:26.157
- PC culture. Man, it's, uh...
- It's (beep) exhausting, bro.

00:10:26.157 --> 00:10:27.050
- Right, bro. It's (beep) out of control.

00:10:27.050 --> 00:10:29.200
- It's exhausting.
- It's just...

00:10:29.200 --> 00:10:30.510
Our culture has been taken over

00:10:30.510 --> 00:10:33.260
by a group of SJW feminist freaks-

00:10:33.260 --> 00:10:34.920
- Feminists, yeah.
- who, uh, who...

00:10:34.920 --> 00:10:37.510
- That must be hard, to be a comedian.

00:10:40.596 --> 00:10:41.760
I feel really bad for him.

00:10:41.760 --> 00:10:43.850
- We as comedians are philosophers.

00:10:43.850 --> 00:10:44.683
All right?
