- Okay, so Burnham's podcast bro

00:10:53.710 --> 00:10:56.330
or podcast bros persona,

00:10:56.330 --> 00:10:58.330
it says something that
gets thrown around a lot,

00:10:58.330 --> 00:11:02.220
which is "We as comedians
are philosophers,"

00:11:02.220 --> 00:11:04.610
and as someone whose two
favorite cultural pursuits

00:11:04.610 --> 00:11:05.940
are philosophy and comedy,

00:11:05.940 --> 00:11:07.760
I've long been both fascinated by

00:11:07.760 --> 00:11:09.980
and skeptical of this claim.

00:11:09.980 --> 00:11:12.570
It's something that usually
gets said by comedians

00:11:12.570 --> 00:11:15.220
who are defending themselves
after being criticized

00:11:15.220 --> 00:11:18.340
for saying deemed
problematic or offensive.

00:11:18.340 --> 00:11:20.890
And the pattern, you know,
usually goes something like,

00:11:20.890 --> 00:11:23.880
comedian says something racist
or sexist or transphobic

00:11:23.880 --> 00:11:25.750
and audiences get mad at them

00:11:25.750 --> 00:11:27.430
and then the comedians and their fans say,

00:11:27.430 --> 00:11:29.210
as a philosopher, it's their, you know,

00:11:29.210 --> 00:11:32.020
job to push the limits and
hold a mirror to society.

00:11:32.020 --> 00:11:34.560
Now, it is true that lots of philosophers

00:11:34.560 --> 00:11:38.340
have been persecuted, critiqued, canceled

00:11:38.340 --> 00:11:41.320
by governments, by the
church, by the media.

00:11:41.320 --> 00:11:42.810
Socrates got the death penalty.

00:11:42.810 --> 00:11:44.900
Spinoza got exiled.

00:11:44.900 --> 00:11:47.040
Kierkegaard was ridiculed in the press.

00:11:47.040 --> 00:11:49.160
Marx got kicked out of
his home country, right?

00:11:49.160 --> 00:11:53.000
So this makes the two Bos
just like those guys, right?

00:11:53.000 --> 00:11:53.833
No, of course not.

00:11:53.833 --> 00:11:54.690
Because for the most part,

00:11:54.690 --> 00:11:57.480
when philosophers have made people upset,

00:11:57.480 --> 00:11:59.240
it's by speaking truth to power

00:11:59.240 --> 00:12:00.690
or what comedians might call

00:12:00.690 --> 00:12:03.250
punching up and not punching down.

00:12:03.250 --> 00:12:06.080
So when Socrates questioned
Athens' rich and powerful

00:12:06.080 --> 00:12:08.050
or Spinoza asked questions
about the nature of God

00:12:08.050 --> 00:12:11.430
or Marx criticized how industrial
labor affects humanity,

00:12:11.430 --> 00:12:12.530
they weren't taking shots

00:12:12.530 --> 00:12:14.430
at already-marginalized groups, right?

00:12:14.430 --> 00:12:17.900
They were poking at the
ideologies used by those in power,

00:12:17.900 --> 00:12:20.410
so the state, the church, the factory.

00:12:20.410 --> 00:12:21.500
Now, another thing you've might've noticed

00:12:21.500 --> 00:12:23.590
is right when they started
talking about this,

00:12:23.590 --> 00:12:26.200
like, comedians-as-philosopher idea,

00:12:26.200 --> 00:12:30.480
an ad came on the screen
for Manstuff Dick Spray,

00:12:30.480 --> 00:12:33.320
and I think it's both very
funny as a name for a product

00:12:33.320 --> 00:12:35.060
but also intentional, right?

00:12:35.060 --> 00:12:36.150
Just as they're talking

00:12:36.150 --> 00:12:39.000
about being history-changing
cultural figures,

00:12:39.000 --> 00:12:42.210
we see that they're also
selling dick spray to make money

00:12:42.210 --> 00:12:44.120
while they're doing their philosophy,

00:12:44.120 --> 00:12:46.050
and this does make me think
of the Greek sophists,

00:12:46.050 --> 00:12:47.890
who Plato and Socrates hated,

00:12:47.890 --> 00:12:50.730
because they offered their
philosophical services

00:12:50.730 --> 00:12:53.600
at a price, and for people
like Plato and Socrates,

00:12:53.600 --> 00:12:57.670
the idea that wisdom could be
purchased was very, very sus.

00:12:57.670 --> 00:12:59.440
Okay, the podcast guys were intense.

00:12:59.440 --> 00:13:03.810
Let's see a more sensitive and
introspective version of Bo

00:13:03.810 --> 00:13:04.730
in this next clip.

00:13:04.730 --> 00:13:09.500
- Since I started so young,

00:13:09.500 --> 00:13:13.563
basically every (beep) joke,

00:13:14.830 --> 00:13:18.400
every sort of misstep is, is, um...

00:13:20.000 --> 00:13:20.980
There's a lot of material

00:13:20.980 --> 00:13:23.300
that I'm just really embarrassed by

00:13:23.300 --> 00:13:27.250
and makes me cringe for a lot of reasons,

00:13:27.250 --> 00:13:30.400
and, uh, just edgy, stupid...
- Okay, so as you see,

00:13:30.400 --> 00:13:32.450
Bo's being very earnest here,

00:13:32.450 --> 00:13:33.840
talking about how hard it's been

00:13:33.840 --> 00:13:36.770
to have so much of his
life documented online.

00:13:36.770 --> 00:13:39.393
- I often wish that I could just,

00:13:41.240 --> 00:13:46.240
you know, start over and
without all the... (sighs)

00:13:48.000 --> 00:13:51.910
- What's most interesting to
me is what pops up onscreen,

00:13:51.910 --> 00:13:54.580
and it's a thumbnail to
click a link to watch a video

00:13:54.580 --> 00:13:59.200
called "OMG! DID I JUST (beep)
A MINECRAFT PINEAPPLE???"

00:13:59.200 --> 00:14:01.780
with a classic YouTube thumbnail face

00:14:01.780 --> 00:14:05.230
of Bo screaming like that,
and I think both this

00:14:05.230 --> 00:14:07.760
and the dick spray ad in the podcast thing

00:14:07.760 --> 00:14:09.450
is bringing up this question, right?

00:14:09.450 --> 00:14:12.950
Can a earnest desire

00:14:12.950 --> 00:14:16.270
for meaning or self-exploration coexist

00:14:16.270 --> 00:14:18.680
with trying to sell (beep) or just get you

00:14:18.680 --> 00:14:20.870
to give more of your
attention away, right?

00:14:20.870 --> 00:14:23.730
Can truth and commerce
coexist in a genuine way?

00:14:23.730 --> 00:14:25.120
And I think in doing this,

00:14:25.120 --> 00:14:28.450
Burnham's both pointing at himself

00:14:28.450 --> 00:14:30.960
as well as a lot of other
people who make content,

00:14:30.960 --> 00:14:34.010
maybe on this website,
on YouTube, right here.

00:14:34.010 --> 00:14:35.010
Okay, enough about that.

00:14:35.010 --> 00:14:37.110
Let's see what he has
to say about the future.

00:14:37.110 --> 00:14:41.839
- The future is in front of now,

00:14:41.839 --> 00:14:42.940
do you know what I mean?

00:14:42.940 --> 00:14:45.133
- The future is in front of now.

00:14:46.230 --> 00:14:48.033
Yeah, it makes sense.

00:14:48.033 --> 00:14:49.430
It kinda doesn't sound
like it makes sense,

00:14:49.430 --> 00:14:50.263
but then it makes sense.

00:14:50.263 --> 00:14:51.096
And we got another ad.

00:14:51.096 --> 00:14:51.929
I just gotta point it out.

00:14:51.929 --> 00:14:53.900
"Mental Health Awareness
Decade at Kohl's."

00:14:53.900 --> 00:14:58.010
I just realized what this
meant on the last take,

00:14:58.010 --> 00:14:59.730
because to be honest like Bo Burnham,

00:14:59.730 --> 00:15:01.860
this is the second take of this section.

00:15:01.860 --> 00:15:02.693
I'm just being honest with you

00:15:02.693 --> 00:15:03.900
in the way he does in the special.

00:15:03.900 --> 00:15:06.030
And on that take, I
realized that there's a joke

00:15:06.030 --> 00:15:08.370
where it says "All
laceless shoes 60% off."

00:15:08.370 --> 00:15:09.203
It's not even a joke.

00:15:09.203 --> 00:15:10.180
It's just really dark, but there you go.

00:15:10.180 --> 00:15:12.710
Now, we've talked about
the future already in this,

00:15:12.710 --> 00:15:15.470
and here, Burnham's exploring the idea

00:15:15.470 --> 00:15:18.300
that it feels bad
sometimes when we realize

00:15:18.300 --> 00:15:20.430
that the future is gonna be made up

00:15:20.430 --> 00:15:21.830
of all the stuff happening now, right?

00:15:21.830 --> 00:15:23.910
The logical extension of
what's already happening.

00:15:23.910 --> 00:15:24.743
It's like if you're a kid

00:15:24.743 --> 00:15:26.370
and you get home from
school and you're hungry

00:15:26.370 --> 00:15:28.160
and you see the ingredients for dinner

00:15:28.160 --> 00:15:30.340
on the table and you hate them.

00:15:30.340 --> 00:15:32.240
They're the ingredients for
your least favorite meal.

00:15:32.240 --> 00:15:33.840
You know that no matter what,

00:15:33.840 --> 00:15:34.810
your dinner's gonna be made

00:15:34.810 --> 00:15:36.310
of those ingredients you don't like.

00:15:36.310 --> 00:15:39.280
But this is something that
our bud Berardi talks about

00:15:39.280 --> 00:15:41.137
in his book "After the
Future," where he says,

00:15:41.137 --> 00:15:43.600
"The rise of the myth
of the future is rooted

00:15:43.600 --> 00:15:45.770
in modern capitalism and the experience

00:15:45.770 --> 00:15:47.940
of expansion of the economy and knowledge.

00:15:47.940 --> 00:15:49.900
The idea that the future will be better

00:15:49.900 --> 00:15:52.050
than the present is not a natural idea

00:15:52.050 --> 00:15:54.930
but the imaginary effect
of the peculiarity

00:15:54.930 --> 00:15:56.790
of the bourgeois production model."

00:15:56.790 --> 00:15:59.100
Okay, so the idea here is that
there was a certain vision

00:15:59.100 --> 00:16:01.777
of modernity, let's say
around the '50s, that said,

00:16:01.777 --> 00:16:03.710
"Everything's going to get better.

00:16:03.710 --> 00:16:05.540
Industry is gonna be more efficient.

00:16:05.540 --> 00:16:07.660
Technology is gonna become more advanced.

00:16:07.660 --> 00:16:09.450
It's gonna make all of our lives better."

00:16:09.450 --> 00:16:10.750
But according to Berardi,

00:16:10.750 --> 00:16:13.490
what we've seen is the slow
cancellation of the future.

00:16:13.490 --> 00:16:16.350
It started in, like, the '60s and the '70s

00:16:16.350 --> 00:16:18.350
into the '80s and kind
of intensified since,

00:16:18.350 --> 00:16:21.250
and this idea of the slow
cancellation of the future

00:16:21.250 --> 00:16:25.170
basically means that time has stopped.

00:16:25.170 --> 00:16:27.160
We're not really experiencing

00:16:27.160 --> 00:16:29.520
any meaningful cultural progress.

00:16:29.520 --> 00:16:31.540
We don't sense that a
better future is present.

00:16:31.540 --> 00:16:33.290
This uncanny feeling is accompanied

00:16:33.290 --> 00:16:35.167
by what scholar Mark Fisher called

00:16:35.167 --> 00:16:37.650
"a deflation of expectations," right?

00:16:37.650 --> 00:16:41.650
So we kind of stop hoping or
imagining things getting better

00:16:41.650 --> 00:16:43.980
because, like, what's the point?

00:16:43.980 --> 00:16:46.780
Okay, this is where Bo, a few Bos,

00:16:46.780 --> 00:16:50.820
give a sort of State of the
Union on content creation.

00:16:50.820 --> 00:16:52.040
- My name is Bo Burnham,

00:16:52.040 --> 00:16:54.200
I am a lifelong content creator,

00:16:54.200 --> 00:16:55.650
and I am absolutely thrilled

00:16:55.650 --> 00:16:57.040
to be speaking with you tonight.

00:16:57.040 --> 00:16:58.890
- This one's way too real

00:16:58.890 --> 00:17:00.500
'cause people really talk like this.

00:17:00.500 --> 00:17:05.310
And as you see, more Bos
start coming onscreen. Yeah.

00:17:05.310 --> 00:17:07.900
And this one's hard to suss
out because as you see,

00:17:07.900 --> 00:17:09.380
there's eventually three Bos speaking,

00:17:09.380 --> 00:17:12.350
so if there's lines that I
missed that are your favorites,

00:17:12.350 --> 00:17:14.240
please drop them in the comments.

00:17:14.240 --> 00:17:15.700
But he definitely shouts out the names

00:17:15.700 --> 00:17:16.940
of some folks at the beginning,

00:17:16.940 --> 00:17:20.220
Bezos, Dorsey, Zuckerberg,
who are actively involved

00:17:20.220 --> 00:17:22.440
in trying to shape our collective future,

00:17:22.440 --> 00:17:24.190
but the part of this that stands out to me

00:17:24.190 --> 00:17:27.790
is when the third monologuing
Burnham talks about-

00:17:27.790 --> 00:17:31.550
- This steady, formless feeling
that hangs over everything,

00:17:31.550 --> 00:17:34.563
this untamable, aimless urgency,

00:17:35.570 --> 00:17:38.710
this sense that all of this is
going to burst at any moment.

00:17:38.710 --> 00:17:40.660
- the fear of what will
happen when it ends

00:17:40.660 --> 00:17:43.580
and the deeper fear of
never hitting the wall

00:17:43.580 --> 00:17:46.060
and this feeling never
ending, never slowing down,

00:17:46.060 --> 00:17:47.860
an endless and pointless climb

00:17:47.860 --> 00:17:50.310
towards a terrible and dense nothing.

00:17:50.310 --> 00:17:53.720
Okay, and here, it seems like
he's talking about an anxiety

00:17:53.720 --> 00:17:57.490
that's produced by both
a lack of a clear future

00:17:57.490 --> 00:18:00.410
and the fact that the
future is in the hands of,

00:18:00.410 --> 00:18:04.400
you know, the Bezoses and
Dorseys and Zuckerbergs

00:18:04.400 --> 00:18:07.550
of the world, that they
get to determine things.

00:18:07.550 --> 00:18:09.280
So it's either their future

00:18:09.280 --> 00:18:12.720
or a potentially scarier
future made by someone else,

00:18:12.720 --> 00:18:15.570
but in all these cases,
the modernist dream

00:18:15.570 --> 00:18:19.110
of indefinite and endless
progress seems very dead.

00:18:19.110 --> 00:18:20.810
Berardi has this to say about the future.

00:18:20.810 --> 00:18:22.210
He says, "The future becomes a threat

00:18:22.210 --> 00:18:24.800
when the collective
imagination becomes incapable

00:18:24.800 --> 00:18:27.220
of seeing possible alternatives to trends,

00:18:27.220 --> 00:18:31.110
leading to devastation,
increased poverty, and violence.

00:18:31.110 --> 00:18:34.660
The paralysis of the will,
the impossibility of politics

00:18:34.660 --> 00:18:37.700
is the historical context of
today's depression epidemic."

00:18:37.700 --> 00:18:41.060
So again, he's saying when we
both can't imagine a future

00:18:41.060 --> 00:18:43.670
and we feel like we can't
determine that future,

00:18:43.670 --> 00:18:46.023
we feel pretty bad, and
I think it's interesting

00:18:46.023 --> 00:18:49.120
that Bo is talking
about content right here

00:18:49.120 --> 00:18:52.190
'cause there's another quote
from Berardi, you guessed it,

00:18:52.190 --> 00:18:54.580
who says, "Despite all its rhetoric

00:18:54.580 --> 00:18:57.230
of novelty and innovation,
neoliberal capitalism

00:18:57.230 --> 00:18:59.530
has gradually but
systematically deprived artists

00:18:59.530 --> 00:19:02.420
of the resources necessary
to produce the new."

00:19:02.420 --> 00:19:04.130
And again, we see this tension.

00:19:04.130 --> 00:19:06.810
We have Bo Burnham,
someone who seemingly wants

00:19:06.810 --> 00:19:09.770
to create art, talking about what it means

00:19:09.770 --> 00:19:13.170
to try to create art and
meaning in this context,

00:19:13.170 --> 00:19:16.350
this accelerating, hypercapitalist,

00:19:16.350 --> 00:19:19.570
futurist model of digital hell.

00:19:19.570 --> 00:19:20.403
That's a bummer.

00:19:20.403 --> 00:19:21.630
This one's just a bummer.

00:19:21.630 --> 00:19:22.570
Okay, let's keep going.

00:19:22.570 --> 00:19:25.680
- The thing that I'm
writing about isn't ending,

00:19:25.680 --> 00:19:30.160
so it's hard to end whatever this is.

00:19:30.160 --> 00:19:31.690
- Okay, all good things

00:19:31.690 --> 00:19:33.610
and all sad things must come to an end,

00:19:33.610 --> 00:19:36.960
so this is Burnham talking

00:19:36.960 --> 00:19:41.960
about why he's ending
the filming right here.

00:19:42.200 --> 00:19:43.033
I don't know, maybe it's just

00:19:43.033 --> 00:19:43.866
that Netflix called and was like,

00:19:43.866 --> 00:19:45.630
"Dude, we gave you all this money.

00:19:45.630 --> 00:19:47.900
You need to give us a special
or we're gonna sue you."

00:19:47.900 --> 00:19:48.733
This is super important

00:19:48.733 --> 00:19:50.170
and I think ties it together
in a really nice way,

00:19:50.170 --> 00:19:53.370
or ties it together in not
tying it together, right?

00:19:53.370 --> 00:19:55.710
Because he wants something
to make sense of it all,

00:19:55.710 --> 00:19:57.630
a big idea, a theme,

00:19:57.630 --> 00:20:00.360
and I think to both sense of this special

00:20:00.360 --> 00:20:04.100
but to also make sense of
everything happening in the world.

00:20:04.100 --> 00:20:07.030
And remember that Berardi,
we read this quote before,

00:20:07.030 --> 00:20:10.180
says that an inability to
find sense can be tied up

00:20:10.180 --> 00:20:12.950
with depression and exhaustion, you know?

00:20:12.950 --> 00:20:15.870
And Bo here is saying the more he waits

00:20:15.870 --> 00:20:17.380
for an ending that'll tie it all together,

00:20:17.380 --> 00:20:20.090
he just feels (beep),
so he just has to stop,

00:20:20.090 --> 00:20:21.810
to just stop doing it.

00:20:21.810 --> 00:20:25.380
Now, in the Berardi book
I've been quoting a lot,

00:20:25.380 --> 00:20:27.180
he kinda gets to this point as well

00:20:27.180 --> 00:20:29.130
and talks about, how do we stop?

00:20:29.130 --> 00:20:30.200
What do we do?

00:20:30.200 --> 00:20:31.920
If the future is canceled, like,

00:20:31.920 --> 00:20:34.350
do we just hang out or something?

00:20:34.350 --> 00:20:35.183
And he says this.

00:20:35.183 --> 00:20:37.850
He says, "I answer the
question, why resist?

00:20:37.850 --> 00:20:40.620
Why persist in seeking
autonomy from power?

00:20:40.620 --> 00:20:41.640
Where is the hope?

00:20:41.640 --> 00:20:45.040
The hope is in the limits of
my knowledge and understanding.

00:20:45.040 --> 00:20:48.200
My knowledge and understanding
do not show the possibility

00:20:48.200 --> 00:20:50.370
of any development of
the present catastrophe

00:20:50.370 --> 00:20:51.920
in agreement with social well-being.

00:20:51.920 --> 00:20:54.400
But the catastrophe is exactly the point

00:20:54.400 --> 00:20:56.910
where a new landscape
is going to be revealed.

00:20:56.910 --> 00:20:59.450
I must resist simply because I cannot know

00:20:59.450 --> 00:21:00.860
what will happen after the future.

00:21:00.860 --> 00:21:02.570
And I must preserve the consciousness

00:21:02.570 --> 00:21:04.480
and sensibility of social solidary,

00:21:04.480 --> 00:21:06.330
of human empathy, of gratuitous activity,

00:21:06.330 --> 00:21:08.100
of freedom, equality, and fraternity.

00:21:08.100 --> 00:21:09.500
Just in case, right?"

00:21:09.500 --> 00:21:11.670
So the optimism here is basically,

00:21:11.670 --> 00:21:14.530
even if in the consciousness we have now,

00:21:14.530 --> 00:21:16.260
we can't see things getting better,

00:21:16.260 --> 00:21:18.300
if all our logic and reason say

00:21:18.300 --> 00:21:20.420
things are gonna keep getting pretty bad,

00:21:20.420 --> 00:21:24.230
and there's some good stuff in
the "Inside" special itself,

00:21:24.230 --> 00:21:26.760
like the "Funny Feeling" song,
that are kind of about that,

00:21:26.760 --> 00:21:29.090
but Berardi's saying
after the catastrophe,

00:21:29.090 --> 00:21:30.780
after the bad thing happens,

00:21:30.780 --> 00:21:32.690
a new landscape's gonna be revealed,

00:21:32.690 --> 00:21:35.140
so we have to hope for that.

00:21:35.140 --> 00:21:37.990
And when he says, you know,
"Just in case, right?",

00:21:37.990 --> 00:21:40.340
he kinda says we gotta keep
hoping, keep resisting,

00:21:40.340 --> 00:21:43.350
keep trying, because what
else are we gonna do?

00:21:43.350 --> 00:21:44.630
And I think there's a little bit

00:21:44.630 --> 00:21:46.190
of a similarity with
what Burnham is doing.

00:21:46.190 --> 00:21:48.820
Okay, one last thing I wanna look at.

00:21:48.820 --> 00:21:50.550
I promise, just one last thing

00:21:50.550 --> 00:21:51.790
and then we'll call this a day.

00:21:51.790 --> 00:21:54.730
Okay, so I just wanna look
at the final image we have

00:21:54.730 --> 00:21:56.714
in the whole outtakes special.

00:21:56.714 --> 00:21:58.550
(waves crashing)

00:21:58.550 --> 00:22:01.540
So it's not a pop-up
telling us to go to Netflix

00:22:01.540 --> 00:22:03.210
and watch his special.

00:22:03.210 --> 00:22:06.280
It's a few seconds of the
ocean hitting the shore,

